            text = "In an era of accelerating climate volatility characterized by localized cloudbursts, severe urban waterlogging, and intensifying cyclonic depressions, the India Meteorological Department (IMD) has deployed an extensive network of S-band and X-band Doppler Weather Radars (DWR). Operating on the physical principle of the Doppler shift, these radars emit high-frequency microwave pulses that bounce off hydrometeors (water droplets, ice crystals, and hail), measuring both the echo intensity and the radial velocity of atmospheric wind fields within a four-hundred-kilometer radius. Advanced dual-polarization technology transmits both horizontal and vertical wave polarizations, enabling meteorologists to distinguish between liquid rain, freezing hail, and non-meteorological airborne debris like migrating birds or dust storms. When integrated with geostationary meteorological satellites such as INSAT-3D and INSAT-3DR, and high-performance supercomputing clusters running Global Forecast Models (GFM) at 12-kilometer grid resolution, the radar network enables hyper-local 'Nowcasting' with lead times of three to six hours. These precise early warnings are broadcast to state disaster management authorities through automated SMS gateways and the Common Alerting Protocol (CAP), successfully reducing cyclone mortality in vulnerable coastal districts by more than ninety percent over the past two decades."
